  "N10" [label="(19,0,0) bZIP73 promotes sugar transport to pollen at low temperatures"];
  "N11" [label="(20,0,0) CTB4a increases energy supply to improve seed setting at high altitudes"];
  "N12" [label="(21,0,0) CTB2 maintains cell membrane permeability under cold stress"];
  "N13" [label="(22,0,0) Precise molecular roles of Ctb1, qCTB7, OsLEA9 remain unclear"];
  "N15" [label="(25,0,0) AtSR1, AtCAMTA1/2/5 positively regulate freezing tolerance and activate cold-inducible genes in Arabidopsis"];
  "N18" [label="(31,0,0) Whether CAMTA family transcription factors play a role in rice cold acclimatization remains unknown"];
  "N21" [label="(0,0,0) Implicit: If a gene family regulates stress responses in model plants, then homologous genes may regulate similar stresses in crop plants"];
  "N26" [label="(0,0,0) Induction: Given multiple known cold tolerance genes at booting stage (bZIP73, CTB4a, CTB2) have diverse molecular mechanisms (sugar transport, energy supply, membrane maintenance), therefore cold tolerance at booting stage likely involves multiple molecular pathways"];
  "N29" [label="(0,0,0) Deduction: Given CAMTA genes regulate cold tolerance in Arabidopsis (from N15), and if gene families have conserved functions across species (from N21), then rice CAMTA genes may regulate cold tolerance"];
  "N6" [label="(7,0,0) Cold injury at booting stage leads to spikelet sterility, lower seed setting, and yield decrease"];
  "N7" [label="(9,0,0) Cold tolerance is controlled by multiple genes at various developmental stages"];
  "N8" [label="(17,0,0) Understanding molecular mechanisms of cold tolerance at booting stage is crucial due to direct impact on grain yield"];
  "N9" [label="(18,0,0) Fewer genes for cold tolerance at booting stage have been identified"];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This paper aims to identify and mechanistically evaluate CTB3, a CAMTA/calmodulin-binding transcription factor carrying 57-bp and 284-bp deletions introgressed from wild rice, as a contributor to japonica rice cold tolerance at the booting stage, testing whether reduced repression by OsTCP19 enables CTB3 activation of OsTPP1 to lower Tre6P, up-regulate sugar-transporter genes, increase panicle sugar accumulation, and support adaptation to higher-latitude cold environments.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When booting-stage cold tolerance is agronomically important, known mechanisms are incomplete, and CAMTA-family genes are plausible cold-response candidates, these conclusions jointly motivate a focused mechanistic study of a CAMTA-related cold-tolerance gene in rice."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Cold injury at the booting stage is an important rice-yield problem, so cold tolerance at this stage is biologically and agronomically important."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) The limited number of identified booting-stage cold-tolerance genes, together with the importance of understanding this stage, motivates identifying additional genes for booting-stage cold tolerance."];
  "R6" [label="(0,0,0) Because precise molecular roles of several booting-stage cold-tolerance loci remain unclear, there is a knowledge gap in understanding molecular mechanisms of cold tolerance at the booting stage."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) CAMTA family transcription factors are plausible candidates to investigate for rice cold acclimatization because CAMTA genes regulate freezing tolerance in Arabidopsis and their role in rice cold acclimatization remains unknown."];
  "R8" [label="(0,0,0) Rice CAMTA genes are reasonable targets for investigation as possible contributors to booting-stage cold tolerance."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) The available motivation supports a research proposal to investigate rice CAMTA-family genes as regulators of booting-stage cold tolerance and to clarify their molecular mechanisms."];
